# Accepted gitleaks findings, by fingerprint.
#
# Per the design rule in .gitleaks.toml: classes of false positive belong in that
# file, individual accepted instances belong here.
#
# All six below are the generic-api-key heuristic firing on long hex strings in
# documentation. Reviewed one by one on 2026-09-22, before the first public release.
#
# WHY THEY ARE NOT SECRETS
#
#   key_fingerprint, first_seen_key_fingerprint, ffiec.chain.key_fingerprint and
#   key_fingerprint_v1_hex are FINGERPRINTS: truncated hashes over a PUBLIC key.
#   Publishing one is the point of having one. It tells an examiner which signing
#   key sealed a chain so the signature can be checked without contacting the
#   institution. A fingerprint discloses nothing about the private key. If these
#   were absent from the documentation the spec would be harder to implement.
#
#   docs/auditor-stories/ holds written scenarios about a FICTIONAL institution,
#   Northbridge Federal Savings. Every identifier in them is invented for the
#   narrative, the transactionKey value included.
#
#   docs/soc-pack/ and docs/feedback/ carry sample event JSON and review notes
#   built from the same synthetic corpus.
#
# The four files carrying these values were moved to the local-only _wip/ tree on
# 2026-09-22, so the working tree no longer contains them. The findings stay
# reachable in history, which is why this file exists: a reader who runs gitleaks
# against the public repo finds the same six, and finds the reason here.

